      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order yet. Amlodipine 20 mg daily is double the FDA-labeled maximum of 10 mg once daily. Doses above 10 mg add little blood pressure benefit and raise the risk of peripheral edema, hypotension, and reflex tachycardia.\n\nIs the 20 mg a typo? Here are some options:\n\n- **Amlodipine 10 mg PO once daily.** This is the max standard dose.\n- **Amlodipine 5 mg PO once daily.** This is the usual starting dose, and 2.5 mg is used for frail patients.\n- **Another antihypertensive.** If 10 mg isn't controlling BP, adding an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide-like diuretic is usually preferred over going past the max.\n\nIf you do want 20 mg, for example because of a specialist recommendation, tell me and I'll enter it with a note documenting the rationale. Otherwise, tell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
